===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|botel|t=bottle, flask, wineskin}}, from {{der|en|fro|boteille}} (Modern French {{m|fr|bouteille}}), from {{der|en|ML.|butticula}}, ultimately of disputed origin. Probably a diminutive of {{der|en|LL.|buttis}}. Compare also {{cog|nds|Buddel}} and {{cog|goh|būtil}} (whence {{cog|de|Beutel}}). {{doublet|en|botija}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A container, typically made of glass or plastic and having a tapered neck, used primarily for holding liquids.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1913|author={{w|Joseph C. Lincoln}}|chapter=6
|title=[http://openlibrary.org/works/OL5535161W Mr. Pratt's Patients]
|passage=He had one hand on the bounce '''bottle'''—and he’d never let go of that since he got back to the table—but he had a handkerchief in the other and was swabbing his deadlights with it.}}
#: {{ux|en|Beer is often sold in '''bottles'''.}}
# The contents of such a container.
#: {{ux|en|I only drank a '''bottle''' of beer.}}
# A container with a rubber nipple used for giving liquids to infants, a [[baby bottle]].
#: {{ux|en|The baby wants a '''bottle'''.}}
# {{lb|en|British|informal}} (originally "bottle and glass" meaning "ass") [[nerve|Nerve]], [[courage]].
#: {{ux|en|You don’t have the '''bottle''' to do that!}}
#: {{ux|en|He was going to ask her out, but he lost his '''bottle''' when he saw her.}}
# {{lb|en|attributive|of a person with a particular hair color}} A container of hair [[dye]], hence with one’s [[hair]] color produced by [[dye]]ing.
#: {{ux|en|Did you know he’s a '''bottle''' brunette? His natural hair color is strawberry blonde.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A bundle, especially of hay; something tied in a bundle.
#* '''End of the 14th century''', {{w|The Canterbury Tales}}, by {{w|Geoffrey Chaucer}}, [[s:The Canterbury Tales/The Manciple's Prologue and Tale|The Manciple’s Prologue and Tale]]
#*: Is that a Cook of London, with mischance?&nbsp;/ Do him come forth, he knoweth his penance;&nbsp;/ For he shall tell a tale, by my [[fay]],&nbsp;/ Although it be not worth a '''bottle''' hay.
#* {{RQ:Marlowe Doctor Faustus|scene=XI|pages=144 and 146|pageref=146|passage=I was no sooner in the middle of the pond, but my horse vanisht away, and I sat vpon a '''bottle''' of [[hay|hey]], neuer so neare drowning in my life: {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Much Ado About Nothing|act=I|scene=i|passage={{smallcaps|Don Pedro}}. Well, if ever thou dost fall from this faith, thou wilt prove a notable argument. / {{smallcaps|Benedick}}. If I do, hang me in a '''bottle''' like a cat and shoot at me; and he that hits me, let him be clapped on the shoulder and called Adam.}}
#* {{RQ:Kingsley Alton Locke|passage=Can you deny that you've been off and on lately between flunkydom and The Cause, like a donkey between two '''bottles''' of hay?}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} Intoxicating liquor; [[alcohol]].
#: {{ux|en|to drown one’s troubles in the '''bottle'''}}
#: {{ux|en|to hit the '''bottle'''}}
#* {{quote-song|en|author={{w|Tracy Chapman}}|album=[[w:Tracy Chapman (album)|Tracy Chapman]]|title={{w|Fast Car}}|date=5 April 1988|passage=See, my old man’s got a problem / He live with the '''bottle''', that’s the way it is}}

